"{\"eval_set_result_id\":\"TravelPlannerAgent_Evaluation_test_1_1763640842.8586402\",\"eval_set_result_name\":\"TravelPlannerAgent_Evaluation_test_1_1763640842.8586402\",\"eval_set_id\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_case_results\":[{\"eval_set_file\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_set_id\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_id\":\"Test2\",\"final_eval_status\":2,\"eval_metric_results\":null,\"overall_eval_metric_results\":[{\"metric_name\":\"tool_trajectory_avg_score\",\"threshold\":1.0,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":1.0,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}},{\"metric_name\":\"response_match_score\",\"threshold\":1.0,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":0.6048733874820832,\"eval_status\":2,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}}],\"eval_metric_result_per_invocation\":[{\"actual_invocation\":{\"invocation_id\":\"e-1b6fb806-c081-44e0-b38a-4911b334db1d\",\"user_content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"final_response\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"ITINERARY: ItineraryBuilderAgent is generating the 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary for Kyoto based on the provided research.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive: Kyoto Itinerary (Optimized)\\n\\nThis itinerary is designed to maximize your cultural immersion, balancing major UNESCO sites with quiet moments of reflection in nature and dedication to traditional arts like the tea ceremony.\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Area\\n\\n**Recommendation:** Stay near the **Kawaramachi/Gion** area or the **Nakagyo Ward**.\\n*   **Why:** This area offers excellent access to the Higashiyama district (Day 2), is central for bus routes, and provides a great evening atmosphere with numerous dining options and access to Gion. It offers a good mix of modern convenience and traditional charm.\\n\\n## Budget Estimates (Per Person, Excluding International Flights)\\n\\n| Expense Category | Estimated Daily Cost (Mid-Range) | Total 7-Day Estimate | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation** | \u00a512,000 \u2013 \u00a518,000 | \u00a584,000 \u2013 \u00a5126,000 | Based on mid-range hotels or high-quality Ryokans. |\\n| **Food & Drink** | \u00a56,000 \u2013 \u00a510,000 | \u00a542,000 \u2013 \u00a570,000 | Includes one nicer dinner and casual lunches/breakfasts. |\\n| **Local Transport** | \u00a51,000 \u2013 \u00a51,500 | \u00a57,000 \u2013 \u00a510,500 | Covers daily bus passes, subway rides, and the Uji trip. |\\n| **Entrance Fees** | \u00a51,500 \u2013 \u00a52,500 | \u00a510,500 \u2013 \u00a517,500 | Covers major temple entry fees. |\\n| **Cultural Experiences** | Variable (Estimate: \u00a57,000) | \u00a57,000 \u2013 \u00a515,000 | Allocating budget for one high-quality Tea Ceremony experience. |\\n| **TOTAL ESTIMATE** | **\u00a527,500 \u2013 \u00a539,000** | **\u00a5150,500 \u2013 \u00a5239,000 (~$1,000 \u2013 $1,600 USD)** | *Highly dependent on dining choices and accommodation style.* |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Day-by-Day Cultural Itinerary\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival and Spiritual Ascent (Southern Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrival at KIX/ITM, transfer to Kyoto Station (Haruka Express recommended), check into accommodation. | 3 hours | Purchase an **ICOCA card** immediately for easy transit use. |\\n| **Lunch** | Casual lunch near Kyoto Station. Try *Kyoto-style ramen* (richer broth). | 1 hour | **Dining Suggestion:** Ichiran or a local shop near the station. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Travel to **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine**. Ascend through the lower *torii* gates. | 3 \u2013 4 hours | **Etiquette Tip:** Bow upon entering the shrine area. Keep noise levels down. |\\n| **Late Afternoon** | Continue ascending as far as you feel comfortable. The crowds thin out the higher you go. | Included | Time your descent for the soft evening light. |\\n| **Evening** | Dinner and local exploration near your accommodation or in the lively Pontocho Alley. | 2 hours | **Dining Suggestion:** Try *Kushikatsu* (deep-fried skewers) in Pontocho. |\\n\\n### Day 2: The Heart of Tradition (Eastern Kyoto / Higashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Early Morning** | Visit **Kiyomizu-dera Temple**. Go right at opening (usually 6:00 AM or 9:00 AM depending on season). | 1.5 hours | Essential to beat crowds. Enjoy the view from the wooden stage. |\\n| **Late Morning** | Walk the historic streets of **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka**. | 2 hours | **Activity:** Browse traditional crafts, pottery, and sweets (e.g., Yatsuhashi). Stop for a matcha soft-serve. |\\n| **Lunch** | Traditional set lunch (*Teishoku*) in Higashiyama. | 1 hour | **Dining Suggestion:** Look for restaurants offering *obanzai* (Kyoto home cooking). |\\n| **Afternoon** | Explore **Kodai-ji Temple** (known for its beautiful gardens) or visit the nearby **Yasaka Shrine**. | 2 hours | Quiet reflection time after the busy main streets. |\\n| **Evening** | Stroll through the **Gion District** (Hanami-koji street). | 2 hours | **Etiquette Reminder:** Do not stop or accost Maiko/Geiko for photos. Observe respectfully from a distance. |\\n| **Dinner** | Enjoy *Kyoto-style Kaiseki* (traditional multi-course dinner) if budget allows, or look for specialized eel restaurants (*unagi*). | 2 hours | **Reservation Highly Recommended for Kaiseki.** |\\n\\n### Day 3: Zen, Gold, and Contemplation (Northwest Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel to **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)**. Visit immediately upon opening. | 1.5 hours | The reflection across the pond is best viewed in the morning sun. |\\n| **Midday** | Travel to **Ryoan-ji Temple**. Contemplate the famous rock garden. | 1.5 hours | **Culture Focus:** Spend time sitting silently, observing the garden\u2019s abstract design. |\\n| **Lunch** | Vegetarian Buddhist cuisine (*Shojin Ryori*) near the temples. | 1 hour | **Dining Suggestion:** Look for *Shojin Ryori* restaurants around the Daitoku-ji complex for an authentic Zen meal. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Explore the **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex**. This area is known for its many sub-temples and links to tea culture. | 2.5 hours | **Optional:** Visit a smaller sub-temple for a quiet break. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to Central Kyoto. Casual dinner and early rest after a morning of travel. | 2 hours | **Dining Suggestion:** Explore the Nishiki Market area for small, quick eats before they close, or grab Udon noodles. |\\n\\n### Day 4: Nature Immersion in Arashiyama (Western Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Early Morning** | Take the JR Sagano Line to Saga-Arashiyama Station. Head straight to the **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove**. | 1.5 hours | **Crucial Tip:** Arrive before 8:30 AM for the best, quietest experience. |\\n| **Mid-Morning** | Visit **Tenryu-ji Temple**, exploring its extensive, beautiful strolling garden. | 1.5 hours | The garden connects directly to the Bamboo Grove path. |\\n| **Lunch** | Lunch near the Katsura River. | 1 hour | **Dining Suggestion:** Try restaurants specializing in Tofu dishes, a regional specialty. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Walk across the **Togetsukyo Bridge**. Visit **Okochi Sanso Villa** for stunning panoramic garden views (requires a small hike). | 2.5 hours | The villa entry includes tea, making it a pleasant break. |\\n| **Late Afternoon** | Optional: Take the scenic **Sagano Romantic Train** (seasonal) or visit the quiet **Adashino Nenbutsu-ji Temple**. | 2 hours |\\n| **Dinner** | Dinner back in central Kyoto or near your hotel. | 1.5 hours | Try *Okonomiyaki* (savory pancake) for a hearty, fun meal. |\\n\\n### Day 5: Imperial History and Philosophical Reflection\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Visit the **Kyoto Imperial Palace (Kyoto Gosho)** grounds. (Check reservation requirements, though often grounds are open access). | 2 hours | Reflect on the sheer scale of the former seat of power. |\\n| **Late Morning** | Travel to the **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku no Michi)**. | 1 hour | Start at the northern end near Ginkaku-ji and walk south. |\\n| **Lunch** | Simple lunch along the Path or near the southern end. | 1 hour | Grab a *bento box* to enjoy riverside if the weather is nice. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Visit **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)**. Focus on its unique dry sand garden (*Ginshadan*). | 1.5 hours | A contrast to Kinkaku-ji\u2014celebrating subtle beauty (*wabi-sabi*). |\\n| **Late Afternoon** | Explore the **Heian Jingu Shrine**, known for its immense bright red *torii* gate and vast garden. | 1.5 hours |\\n| **Dinner** | Explore the *depachika* (basement food halls) of a major department store (like Takashimaya) for an incredible variety of high-quality takeout. | 2 hours | A fantastic, high-quality budget dinner option. |\\n\\n### Day 6: Shoguns, Tea Ceremony Immersion\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Visit **Nijo Castle**. Focus on the Ninomaru Palace and its \\\"nightingale floors\\\" (squeaking security mechanism). | 2.5 hours | **Culture Focus:** Observe the opulent decoration symbolizing Shogun power, contrasted with the austerity of the temples. |\\n| **Lunch** | Quick lunch near Nijo Castle or back toward the city center. | 1 hour |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Dedicated Tea Ceremony Experience.** (Pre-booked essential). | 2 \u2013 3 hours | **Highlight:** Choose a ceremony focused on the philosophy behind *Chanoyu*. Follow all instructions from the host meticulously. |\\n| **Late Afternoon** | **Optional:** Visit **Kennin-ji Temple** (oldest Zen temple in Kyoto) near Gion for its beautiful Zen artwork. | 1.5 hours |\\n| **Dinner** | Farewell dinner reflecting your favorite Kyoto cuisine discovered this week (e.g., return to a favorite ramen spot or try a specialized *shabu-shabu*). | 2 hours | Use this evening to practice a few Japanese phrases learned during the week. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Matcha Pilgrimage and Departure (Uji Day Trip)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel via Keihan or JR Nara Line to **Uji City** (approx. 20-30 minutes from Kyoto). | 1 hour | Uji is the center of premium matcha production. |\\n| **Late Morning** | Visit **Byodo-in Temple** (featured on the 10-yen coin). | 1.5 hours | Admire the Phoenix Hall architecture. |\\n| **Lunch** | Lunch centered entirely around **Matcha**. | 1.5 hours | **Dining Suggestion:** Try *Matcha Soba* noodles or rich *Matcha Parfaits* at a famous local tea house (e.g., Nakamura Tokichi Honten). |\\n| **Afternoon** | Purchase high-quality loose-leaf tea or matcha souvenirs in Uji. Enjoy a final, calming cup of expertly whisked tea. | 2 hours |\\n| **Late Afternoon/Evening**| Return to Kyoto Station. Final souvenir shopping or direct transit to the airport for departure. | Varies | Allow ample time for airport transfer. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n# TravelOptimizerAgent: Optimization & Practical Guidance\\n\\n## Money-Saving Tips and Budget Alternatives\\n\\n1.  **Embrace the Bus Pass:** For Days 2, 3, 4, and 5, if you anticipate more than three bus rides, purchase the **Kyoto City Bus One-Day Pass (\u00a5700)**. This saves significant money compared to paying the \u00a5230 flat fare multiple times daily.\\n2.  **Temple Hopping Strategy:** Purchase entrance tickets on-site; do not pre-buy multi-temple passes unless you are absolutely certain you will visit every site listed on it. Prioritize **one** major, expensive temple (like Kiyomizu-dera) and balance it with several free or low-cost shrines (like Yasaka Shrine or Fushimi Inari).\\n3.  **Lunch Savings (Obanzai & Depachika):** Maximize savings during the middle of the day. Lunch *Teishoku* sets are significantly cheaper than dinner versions. On Day 5, leverage the *Depachika* (department store food basements) for gourmet, high-quality takeout meals that are often discounted an hour before closing.\\n4.  **Fushimi Inari:** Ascending past the main viewing areas dramatically cuts down on tourist congestion *and* allows you to skip entrance fees entirely, as it is free.\\n5.  **Water Refills:** Carry a reusable bottle. While public water fountains are rare, many mid-range hotels and some restaurants will happily refill your water bottle for free. Vending machine drinks are reliable but add up quickly.\\n\\n## Packing Recommendations Specific to Kyoto\\n\\nKyoto requires comfortable footwear and respectful attire for sacred sites.\\n\\n*   **Footwear:** **Comfortable walking shoes are non-negotiable.** You will average 15,000+ steps daily. Shoes that slip on and off easily (like loafers or sturdy slip-on sneakers) are ideal for the frequent requirement to remove them at temples and traditional restaurants.\\n*   **Layers:** Weather in Spring/Autumn can shift drastically from cool mornings to warm afternoons. Pack light, versatile layers (cardigans, thin waterproof jacket).\\n*   **Modest Attire (For Temples/Ceremonies):** While Kyoto is generally modern, avoid overly revealing clothing (short shorts, spaghetti straps) when visiting temples or attending the tea ceremony. Shoulders and knees should ideally be covered for maximum respect.\\n*   **Small Bag/Backpack:** Essential for carrying water, guidebooks, purchased snacks, and layer removal. Ensure it is secure, especially in crowded areas like Higashiyama.\\n*   **Cash:** While cards are accepted more widely now, many smaller temples, bus fares, and traditional shops (especially outside central Kawaramachi) still prefer or only accept cash (Yen).\\n\\n## Backup Plans for Weather or Unexpected Situations\\n\\n| Situation | Original Plan Impacted | Backup/Contingency Plan |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Heavy Rain/Typhoon Day** | Arashiyama Bamboo Grove (Day 4) / Philosopher's Path (Day 5) | **Focus Indoors:** Spend the day at **Kyoto National Museum** (Day 5 alternative) or the **Kyoto International Manga Museum**. For Day 4, spend extra time exploring the indoor sections of Tenryu-ji Temple or book an indoor workshop (e.g., traditional sweet-making class). |\\n| **Crowd Overload (Peak Season)** | Kiyomizu-dera (Day 2) / Kinkaku-ji (Day 3) | **Shift Times/Seek Quiet:** If crowds are unbearable, visit early morning (before 7:30 AM) or late afternoon (after 4:30 PM). If still too busy, swap a major site for a less-visited nearby gem, such as **Shisendo Temple** instead of Ginkaku-ji for a quieter garden experience. |\\n| **Transportation Strike/Delays** | Uji Day Trip (Day 7) | **Stay Central:** If train lines are affected, skip Uji. Dedicate Day 7 to exploring the **Nishiki Market** in depth, visiting **To-ji Temple** (famous for its five-story pagoda, near Kyoto Station), and purchasing final souvenirs near the station. |\\n| **Tea Ceremony Cancellation** | Day 6 Afternoon | **Flex Cultural Slot:** Book an alternative workshop in advance, such as a **Kimono Dressing experience**, **Sake tasting**, or a **Wagashi (traditional sweets) making class**. |\\n\\n## Local Apps, Websites, or Resources to Download\\n\\n| Resource | Purpose | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Japan Travel by NAVITIME** | Navigation/Transit Planning | Excellent for mapping out multi-step journeys involving buses, subways, and walking directions. Shows real-time transfers. |\\n| **Google Maps** | General Navigation & Reviews | Reliable for finding shop/restaurant locations and checking operating hours (though always double-check opening times for temples). |\\n| **Google Translate (Download Japanese Offline Pack)** | Communication | Essential for menus, signs, and quick conversational help, even without Wi-Fi. |\\n| **Japan Official Travel App (JNTO)** | Tourist Information/Safety | Good for general information, emergency contacts, and sometimes Wi-Fi hotspot locations. |\\n| **A Local Currency Converter** | Budget Tracking | Keep track of spending in Yen vs. your home currency easily. |\\n\\n## Cultural Do's and Don'ts for Respectful Travel\\n\\n### **DO:**\\n\\n*   **DO** use the purification fountain (*temizuya*) correctly at shrines before approaching the main hall (rinse the ladle, then your hands).\\n*   **DO** try to use basic Japanese phrases like *Arigatou gozaimasu* (Thank you) and *Sumimasen* (Excuse me/Pardon me).\\n*   **DO** take note of which temples allow photography and which sections are restricted.\\n*   **DO** walk on the left side of the sidewalk/pathway, matching Japanese driving norms.\\n\\n### **DON'T:**\\n\\n*   **DON'T** eat, drink, or smoke while walking in public areas, especially crowded streets like Sannenzaka or near sacred sites.\\n*   **DON'T** point directly at people or objects with your fingers; use an open hand gesture instead.\\n*   **DON'T** bring large backpacks into small tea rooms or temple halls; use the provided racks or carry them in front of you.\\n*   **DON'T** stop, chase, or block the path of a Geiko or Maiko for photos in Gion; this is highly disrespectful and intrusive. Observe from a discreet distance.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"intermediate_data\":{\"invocation_events\":[]},\"creation_timestamp\":1763640818.274562,\"rubrics\":null,\"app_details\":{\"agent_details\":{\"DestinationResearchAgent\":{\"name\":\"DestinationResearchAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a travel researcher. You will be given a destination and travel preferences, and you will research:\\n    - Best time to visit and weather patterns\\n    - Top attractions and must-see locations\\n    - Local culture, customs, and etiquette tips\\n    - Transportation options within the destination\\n    - Safety considerations and travel requirements\\n    Provide comprehensive destination insights for trip planning.\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"DestinationResearchAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that researches travel destinations and gathers essential information\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[{\"function_declarations\":null,\"retrieval\":null,\"google_search_retrieval\":null,\"computer_use\":null,\"file_search\":null,\"code_execution\":null,\"enterprise_web_search\":null,\"google_maps\":null,\"google_search\":{\"exclude_domains\":null,\"blocking_confidence\":null,\"time_range_filter\":null},\"url_context\":null}]},\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\":{\"name\":\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a professional travel planner. Using the research from \\\"destination_research\\\" output, create a detailed itinerary that includes:\\n    - Day-by-day schedule with recommended activities\\n    - Suggested accommodation areas or districts\\n    - Estimated time requirements for each activity\\n    - Meal recommendations and dining suggestions\\n    - Budget estimates for major expenses\\n    Structure it logically for easy following during the trip.\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that creates structured travel itineraries with daily schedules\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[]},\"TravelOptimizerAgent\":{\"name\":\"TravelOptimizerAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a seasoned travel consultant. Using the itinerary from \\\"travel_itinerary\\\" output, 