*Teishoku*) in Higashiyama. | 1 hour | **Dining Suggestion:** Look for restaurants offering *obanzai* (Kyoto home cooking). |\\n| **Afternoon** | Explore **Kodai-ji Temple** (known for its beautiful gardens) or visit the nearby **Yasaka Shrine**. | 2 hours | Quiet reflection time after the busy main streets. |\\n| **Evening** | Stroll through the **Gion District** (Hanami-koji street). | 2 hours | **Etiquette Reminder:** Do not stop or accost Maiko/Geiko for photos. Observe respectfully from a distance. |\\n| **Dinner** | Enjoy *Kyoto-style Kaiseki* (traditional multi-course dinner) if budget allows, or look for specialized eel restaurants (*unagi*). | 2 hours | **Reservation Highly Recommended for Kaiseki.** |\\n\\n### Day 3: Zen, Gold, and Contemplation (Northwest Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel to **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)**. Visit immediately upon opening. | 1.5 hours | The reflection across the pond is best viewed in the morning sun. |\\n| **Midday** | Travel to **Ryoan-ji Temple**. Contemplate the famous rock garden. | 1.5 hours | **Culture Focus:** Spend time sitting silently, observing the garden\u2019s abstract design. |\\n| **Lunch** | Vegetarian Buddhist cuisine (*Shojin Ryori*) near the temples. | 1 hour | **Dining Suggestion:** Look for *Shojin Ryori* restaurants around the Daitoku-ji complex for an authentic Zen meal. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Explore the **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex**. This area is known for its many sub-temples and links to tea culture. | 2.5 hours | **Optional:** Visit a smaller sub-temple for a quiet break. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to Central Kyoto. Casual dinner and early rest after a morning of travel. | 2 hours | **Dining Suggestion:** Explore the Nishiki Market area for small, quick eats before they close, or grab Udon noodles. |\\n\\n### Day 4: Nature Immersion in Arashiyama (Western Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Early Morning** | Take the JR Sagano Line to Saga-Arashiyama Station. Head straight to the **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove**. | 1.5 hours | **Crucial Tip:** Arrive before 8:30 AM for the best, quietest experience. |\\n| **Mid-Morning** | Visit **Tenryu-ji Temple**, exploring its extensive, beautiful strolling garden. | 1.5 hours | The garden connects directly to the Bamboo Grove path. |\\n| **Lunch** | Lunch near the Katsura River. | 1 hour | **Dining Suggestion:** Try restaurants specializing in Tofu dishes, a regional specialty. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Walk across the **Togetsukyo Bridge**. Visit **Okochi Sanso Villa** for stunning panoramic garden views (requires a small hike). | 2.5 hours | The villa entry includes tea, making it a pleasant break. |\\n| **Late Afternoon** | Optional: Take the scenic **Sagano Romantic Train** (seasonal) or visit the quiet **Adashino Nenbutsu-ji Temple**. | 2 hours |\\n| **Dinner** | Dinner back in central Kyoto or near your hotel. | 1.5 hours | Try *Okonomiyaki* (savory pancake) for a hearty, fun meal. |\\n\\n### Day 5: Imperial History and Philosophical Reflection\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Visit the **Kyoto Imperial Palace (Kyoto Gosho)** grounds. (Check reservation requirements, though often grounds are open access). | 2 hours | Reflect on the sheer scale of the former seat of power. |\\n| **Late Morning** | Travel to the **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku no Michi)**. | 1 hour | Start at the northern end near Ginkaku-ji and walk south. |\\n| **Lunch** | Simple lunch along the Path or near the southern end. | 1 hour | Grab a *bento box* to enjoy riverside if the weather is nice. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Visit **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)**. Focus on its unique dry sand garden (*Ginshadan*). | 1.5 hours | A contrast to Kinkaku-ji\u2014celebrating subtle beauty (*wabi-sabi*). |\\n| **Late Afternoon** | Explore the **Heian Jingu Shrine**, known for its immense bright red *torii* gate and vast garden. | 1.5 hours |\\n| **Dinner** | Explore the *depachika* (basement food halls) of a major department store (like Takashimaya) for an incredible variety of high-quality takeout. | 2 hours | A fantastic, high-quality budget dinner option. |\\n\\n### Day 6: Shoguns, Tea Ceremony Immersion\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Visit **Nijo Castle**. Focus on the Ninomaru Palace and its \\\"nightingale floors\\\" (squeaking security mechanism). | 2.5 hours | **Culture Focus:** Observe the opulent decoration symbolizing Shogun power, contrasted with the austerity of the temples. |\\n| **Lunch** | Quick lunch near Nijo Castle or back toward the city center. | 1 hour |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Dedicated Tea Ceremony Experience.** (Pre-booked essential). | 2 \u2013 3 hours | **Highlight:** Choose a ceremony focused on the philosophy behind *Chanoyu*. Follow all instructions from the host meticulously. |\\n| **Late Afternoon** | **Optional:** Visit **Kennin-ji Temple** (oldest Zen temple in Kyoto) near Gion for its beautiful Zen artwork. | 1.5 hours |\\n| **Dinner** | Farewell dinner reflecting your favorite Kyoto cuisine discovered this week (e.g., return to a favorite ramen spot or try a specialized *shabu-shabu*). | 2 hours | Use this evening to practice a few Japanese phrases learned during the week. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Matcha Pilgrimage and Departure (Uji Day Trip)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel via Keihan or JR Nara Line to **Uji City** (approx. 20-30 minutes from Kyoto). | 1 hour | Uji is the center of premium matcha production. |\\n| **Late Morning** | Visit **Byodo-in Temple** (featured on the 10-yen coin). | 1.5 hours | Admire the Phoenix Hall architecture. |\\n| **Lunch** | Lunch centered entirely around **Matcha**. | 1.5 hours | **Dining Suggestion:** Try *Matcha Soba* noodles or rich *Matcha Parfaits* at a famous local tea house (e.g., Nakamura Tokichi Honten). |\\n| **Afternoon** | Purchase high-quality loose-leaf tea or matcha souvenirs in Uji. Enjoy a final, calming cup of expertly whisked tea. | 2 hours |\\n| **Late Afternoon/Evening**| Return to Kyoto Station. Final souvenir shopping or direct transit to the airport for departure. | Varies | Allow ample time for airport transfer. |\\n\\n    OPTIMIZATION TIPS: [your money-saving and practical tips here]\\n\\n    TRAVEL ESSENTIALS: [packing and preparation advice here]\\n\\n    BACKUP PLANS: [alternative options and contingencies here]\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"TravelOptimizerAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that optimizes travel plans with practical advice and alternatives\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[]}}}},\"expected_invocation\":{\"invocation_id\":\"e-f2ef503f-ae2d-4fe2-b8d4-40c7327c0051\",\"user_content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"final_response\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"As your TravelOptimizerAgent, I have integrated crucial money-saving strategies, specific packing lists, contingency plans, and essential local resources into your detailed 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary.\\n\\n# 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary: Kyoto (Optimized)\\n\\nThis itinerary is designed for a comprehensive cultural immersion in Kyoto, balancing iconic historical sites with intimate experiences of Zen, tea traditions, and natural beauty.\\n\\n**Best Time to Travel:** Spring (Cherry Blossoms) or Autumn (Fall Foliage).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Area\\n\\nFor a first-time deep dive focusing on culture and accessibility, consider staying in or near:\\n\\n1.  **Higashiyama (East Kyoto):** Excellent access to Kiyomizu-dera, Gion, and the Philosopher's Path. Very atmospheric.\\n2.  **Central Kyoto (Near Karasuma/Kawaramachi Stations):** Best for transportation links (subway and buses) and modern dining/shopping convenience.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Day-by-Day Schedule (With Optimizations)\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival & The Iconic East (Higashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Arrival at KIX/ITM, Transfer to Kyoto, Check-in | **BUDGET TIP:** If using KIX, the *Haruka Express* is fast but expensive. Consider the cheaper **JR Local Rapid Service** followed by a transfer, or the **Airport Limousine Bus** for direct, often cheaper service to Kyoto Station. |\\n| Lunch | Simple Udon or Soba near your hotel. | **MONEY SAVER:** Avoid restaurants directly facing major tourist spots (like the street leading to Kiyomizu-dera). Walk 2-3 blocks away for better value. |\\n| Afternoon | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | **CULTURAL DO\u2019s/DON\u2019Ts:** Use the purification fountain (Temizuya) correctly: ladle water, wash left hand, then right, pour water into your cupped left hand to rinse the ladle handle, then discard water. |\\n| Late Afternoon | Stroll **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** streets | **PACKING:** Wear comfortable, slip-on shoes. You will encounter many steep, uneven stone steps and some shops/temples require shoe removal. |\\n| Dinner | **Higashiyama Dinner** | **BACKUP:** If Pontocho/Gion is booked, try the dining options inside the Shijo Kawaramachi department stores (like Takashimaya or Daimaru) for high-quality but cheaper set menus at lunchtime or early dinner. |\\n\\n### Day 2: Zen, Tea, and Geiko Culture\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Traditional Tea Ceremony Experience** | **MONEY SAVER:** Book a group ceremony rather than a private one. Look for ceremonies held in public community centers or smaller temples rather than high-end tourist venues for savings of 30-50%. |\\n| Lunch | Lunch in Gion | **LOCAL APP:** Use **Tabelog** (Japan's primary restaurant review site) to find highly-rated, lower-priced lunch sets (*teishoku*) in the Gion area. |\\n| Afternoon | **Kodai-ji Temple** | **CULTURAL DON\u2019T:** Do not use flash photography anywhere inside temple buildings, even if it's not explicitly forbidden, as it degrades the art and disturbs visitors. |\\n| Evening | **Gion District Walk** | **CRUCIAL DON'T:** Never attempt to stop, touch, or photograph a Geiko/Maiko if they are clearly rushing. Respect their work commute. Stay on public sidewalks. |\\n| Dinner | **Pontocho Alley Dining** | **BACKUP PLAN:** If Pontocho is too busy or expensive, explore the smaller side alleys branching off Shijo Dori for less crowded *yakitori* joints. |\\n\\n### Day 3: Golden Splendor and Contemplation\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | **MONEY SAVER:** Entry fees are fixed, but arriving right at opening (9 AM) avoids paying for entry when the sun is harsh and crowds are thick, maximizing the visual experience for the standard fee. |\\n| Transit | Travel to Ryoan-ji | **TRANSPORT TIP:** Purchase an **ICOCA/Suica Card** immediately. This refillable card streamlines bus/subway travel immensely\u2014no need to fumble for exact change or bus passes daily. |\\n| Afternoon | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | **CULTURAL DO:** Sit quietly for at least 10 minutes at the viewing platform. The garden is designed for quiet contemplation; rushing it defeats the purpose of this Zen stop. |\\n| Late Afternoon | **Nishijin Textile Center (Optional)** | **BUDGET ALTERNATIVE:** If skipping the center, visit a small, independent craft shop in the Nishijin area to see artisans at work without the tourist shop markup. |\\n| Dinner | **Central Kyoto/Nishiki Market Area** | **MONEY SAVER:** Nishiki Market vendors often sell ready-to-eat foods (pickles, mochi, skewers). Grab a selection of these for a delicious, varied, and cheap dinner feast. |\\n\\n### Day 4: The Thousand Gates of Inari\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine** | **BACKUP PLAN (Heat/Rain):** If hiking in extreme heat or heavy rain, only go up to the first major junction (Yotsutsuji intersection) for the famous view, then descend. The upper mountain paths can be exposed. |\\n| Lunch | **Inari Street Food** | **CULTURAL DO:** Trying *Kitsune Udon* (udon noodles with sweet fried tofu) is highly recommended here, as the fox (*kitsune*) is the messenger of the Inari deity. |\\n| Afternoon | Return to Central Kyoto & Rest | **PACKING:** Bring moisture-wicking socks and a small towel. You will sweat while hiking Inari, and local washrooms/sento often charge for towel rental. |\\n| Evening | **Local Izakaya Experience** | **CULTURAL DO:** When ordering at an Izakaya, expect a small cover charge called *ot\u014dshi* or *tsukidashi*\u2014this is mandatory and includes a small appetizer. |\\n\\n### Day 5: Arashiyama Nature Immersion\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Travel to Arashiyama & **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | **MONEY SAVER:** Tenry\u016b-ji has two entry fees: the main hall/garden and the separate Bamboo Grove access. If you are on a tight budget, skip the main hall but walk through the famous **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** (Day 5 PM activity) which is free to access from the back paths. |\\n| Lunch | Riverside Dining | **BUDGET TIP:** Buy high-quality *bento* boxes from a department store basement (*depachika*) near Kyoto Station before heading out, and have a scenic picnic lunch by the river. |\\n| Afternoon | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | **LOCAL RESOURCE:** Download an offline map of Arashiyama. The paths diverge, and good signage is sometimes lacking deeper in the woods. |\\n| Evening | **Relaxation / Onsen** | **PACKING/CULTURAL NOTE:** If visiting a public bath/onsen, you must be comfortable bathing nude (separately for men/women). No tattoos are allowed in many traditional establishments\u2014research tattoo-friendly options like **Tendon no Yu** if this is a concern. |\\n\\n### Day 6: Shogun Power and Secluded Zen\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Nijo-jo Castle** | **CULTURAL DON'T:** Do not step on the edges of the \\\"nightingale floors\\\" as they are more likely to squeak when stepped on near the perimeter. Walk carefully in the center if possible. |\\n| Afternoon | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex** | **ZEN TIP:** Select *one* sub-temple (e.g., Koto-in) to focus on deeply, as paying entry fees for too many small temples drains the budget quickly. The larger Daitoku-ji grounds are free to wander. |\\n| Evening | **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku-no-michi) Walk** | **BACKUP PLAN (Rain):** If it is raining heavily, substitute this walk with a visit to the **Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design** near Miyako Messe for an indoor cultural experience. |\\n| Dinner | **Near Demachiyanagi or Imadegawa** | **MONEY SAVER:** This university-adjacent area (near Kyoto University) has many excellent, cheap, and authentic restaurants catering to students. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Final Masterpieces & Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple** | **CULTURAL DO:** Photography is strictly forbidden inside. Before entering, pause outside to meditate on the sheer scale of the statues; this helps prepare your mind for the overwhelming experience inside. |\\n| Midday | **Final Reflection Spot (Honen-in Temple)** | **PACKING:** Pick up high-quality, long-lasting souvenirs like Kyoto incense (*Oko*) or preserved tea leaves today rather than waiting until the station. |\\n| Lunch | **Kyoto Station Area** | **BUDGET TIP:** Head to the Isetan department store food halls (*depachika*) around 1 PM. Some high-end deli/bakery items are deeply discounted near closing time (usually 6 PM onwards, check local schedules). |\\n| Afternoon | Souvenir Shopping & Departure Prep | **LOCAL APP:** Use **Google Maps** for real-time navigation to the airport transfer point, confirming the final platform/gate number for the Haruka Express or Shinkansen. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Optimization Deep Dive\\n\\n### \ud83d\udcb0 Money-Saving Tips & Budget Alternatives\\n\\n1.  **Transportation Flexibility:** Rely primarily on walking and the efficient bus network. Purchase the **Kyoto City Bus One-Day Pass (700 JPY)** only if you plan 3 or more bus rides. For days using the JR lines (Inari, Arashiyama), an ICOCA/Suica card is best.\\n2.  **Temple Passes:** Most major temples charge between 400\u2013600 JPY entry. If you find yourself consistently skipping afternoon visits due to fatigue or budget strain, prioritize only the UNESCO sites (Kiyomizu, Kinkaku-ji, Tenryu-ji) to save entry fees.\\n3.  **Dining Strategy:** Embrace *Konbini* (convenience stores like 7-Eleven, Lawson, Family Mart) for excellent, fresh breakfasts (Onigiri, Sandwiches) for under 400 JPY. For dinner, prioritize lunch sets (*teishoku*) which offer the same quality as dinner but at half the price.\\n4.  **Water:** Carry a reusable water bottle. Vending machines are everywhere (and cheaper than buying bottled water in stores), and many temples have public water sources for refilling (check signage).\\n\\n### \ud83e\uddf3 Packing Recommendations (Kyoto Specific)\\n\\n*   **Comfortable Footwear:** Non-negotiable. You will easily walk 8-10 miles daily over uneven paving, gravel, and stairs.\\n*   **Layered Clothing:** Evenings can be cool, especially near the rivers or when entering shady temple halls, even if the day is warm. Pack light, breathable layers.\\n*   **Small, Foldable Umbrella/Rain Shell:** Essential, especially during the rainy season (June) or unpredictable shoulder seasons.\\n*   **Small Gift (Omiyage):** If visiting a private tea ceremony or seeking specific artisanal services, having a small, attractively packaged souvenir from your home country (or a standard Japanese gift item) is a very polite gesture.\\n*   **Portable Charger:** Early mornings and late evenings mean relying heavily on navigation apps; keep your phone charged.\\n\\n### \ud83c\udf27\ufe0f Backup Plans for Weather or Unexpected Situations\\n\\n| Situation | Backup Activity / Contingency Plan |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **Heavy Rain/Typhoon Warning** | **Day 5/6 Substitute:** Visit the **Kyoto International Manga Museum** (Central Kyoto), explore the massive underground shopping malls around Kyoto Station, or spend a long afternoon in the **Kyoto National Museum** or the **Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design**. |\\n| **Extreme Heat (Summer)** | Focus on sites with excellent air conditioning (Nijo-jo, Museums). Schedule Fushimi Inari for pre-sunrise (5:30 AM start) and retreat indoors between 11 AM and 3 PM. Utilize public transport heavily. |\\n| **Unexpected Temple Closure** | Many small temples close for maintenance or private ceremonies. **Backup Plan:** Head to the nearby **Shinto Shrines** (which often have different opening hours) or explore local, unnamed neighborhood temples\u2014these are often the most authentic finds. |\\n| **Transportation Strikes/Delays** | If the JR line to Arashiyama is delayed, use the **Randen Tram** instead. It\u2019s slower but offers a lovely, scenic ride through older districts. |\\n\\n### \ud83d\udcf1 Local Apps, Websites, or Resources to Download\\n\\n1.  **Google Maps:** Essential for bus routes, walking times, and train schedules. Turn on \\\"Public Transit\\\" layers.\\n2.  **Japan Transit Planner (Jorudan) or Navitime:** Excellent secondary resources for complex train routes involving multiple carriers (e.g., Shinkansen connections).\\n3.  **Tabelog:** For reliable restaurant reviews and finding local gems away from tourist traps.\\n4.  **Google Translate:** Download the Japanese language pack for offline translation, particularly useful for reading menus or signs.\\n5.  **ICOCA/Suica App:** If you use a digital wallet on your phone, you can load and manage your transport card digitally, eliminating the need for a physical card.\\n\\n### \u26e9\ufe0f Cultural Do's & Don'ts for Respectful Travel\\n\\n| Do | Don't |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **DO** use quiet, respectful tones inside temples, shrines, and on public transport. | **DON'T** eat, drink, or smoke while walking down the historic streets (Sannenzaka, Gion). Find a designated spot. |\\n| **DO** learn a few key phrases: *Arigatou gozaimasu* (Thank you very much) and *Sumimasen* (Excuse me/Sorry). | **DON'T** take pictures of people without asking, especially Geiko/Maiko or people performing religious rites. |\\n| **DO** accept business cards (if exchanged) with both hands and examine them briefly before putting them away respectfully. | **DON'T** leave trash behind. Carry a small plastic bag for wrappers and bottles until you find a trash can. |\\n| **DO** remove your shoes promptly when entering homes, certain temples, and traditional restaurants/tea houses. | **DON'T** point with your index finger; use an open hand gesture instead. |\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"intermediate_data\":{\"tool_uses\":[],\"tool_responses\":[],\"intermediate_responses\":[]},\"creation_timestamp\":1763640594.995979,\"rubrics\":null,\"app_details\":null},\"eval_metric_results\":[{\"metric_name\":\"tool_trajectory_avg_score\",\"threshold\":1.0,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":1.0,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}},{\"metric_name\":\"response_match_score\",\"threshold\":1.0,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":0.6048733874820832,\"eval_status\":2,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}}]}],\"session_id\":\"___eval___session___534cef1b-3186-445c-a7d1-c1e96c0a79c5\",\"session_details\":{\"id\":\"___eval___session___534cef1b-3186-445c-a7d1-c1e96c0a79c5\",\"app_name\":\"TravelPlannerAgent\",\"user_id\":\"user\",\"state\":{\"travel_itinerary\":\"ItineraryBuilderAgent is generating the 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary for Kyoto based on the provided research.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive: Kyoto Itinerary\\n\\nThis itinerary is designed to maximize your cultural immersion, balancing major UNESCO sites with quiet moments of reflection in nature and dedication to traditional arts like the tea ceremony.\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Area\\n\\n**Recommendation:** Stay near the **Kawaramachi/Gion** area or the **Nakagyo Ward**.\\n*   **Why:** This area offers excellent access to the Higashiyama district (Day 2), is central for bus routes, and provides a great evening atmosphere with numerous dining options and access to Gion. It offers a good mix of modern convenience and traditional charm.\\n\\n## Budget Estimates (Per Person, Excluding International Flights)\\n\\n| Expense Category | Estimated Daily Cost (Mid-Range) | Total 7-Day Estimate | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation** | \u00a512,000 \u2013 \u00a518,000 | \u00a584,000 \u2013 \u00a5126,000 | Based on mid-range hotels or high-quality Ryokans. |\\n| **Food & Drink** | \u00a56,000 \u2013 \u00a510,000 | \u00a542,000 \u2013 \u00a570,000 | Includes one nicer dinner and casual lunches/breakfasts. |\\n| **Local Transport** | \u00a51,000 \u2013 \u00a51,500 | \u00a57,000 \u2013 \u00a510,500 | Covers daily bus passes, subway rides, and the Uji trip. |\\n| **Entrance Fees** | \u00a51,500 \u2013 \u00a52,500 | \u00a510,500 \u2013 \u00a517,500 | Covers major temple entry fees. |\\n| **Cultural Experiences** | Variable (Estimate: \u00a57,000) | \u00a57,000 \u2013 \u00a515,000 | Allocating budget for one high-quality Tea Ceremony experience. |\\n| **TOTAL ESTIMATE** | **\u00a527,500 \u2013 \u00a539,000** | **\u00a5150,500 \u2013 \u00a5239,000 (~$1,000 \u2013 $1,600 USD)** | *Highly dependent on dining choices and accommodation style.* |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Day-by-Day Cultural Itinerary\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival and Spiritual Ascent (Southern Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrival at KIX/ITM, transfer to Kyoto Station (Haruka Express recommended), check into accommodation. | 3 hours | Purchase an **ICOCA card** immediately for easy transit use. |\\n| **Lunch** | Casual lunch near Kyoto Station. Try *Kyoto-style ramen* (richer broth). | 1 hour | **Dining Suggestion:** Ichiran or a local shop near the station. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Travel to **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine**. Ascend through the lower *torii* gates. | 3 \u2013 4 hours | **Etiquette Tip:** Bow upon entering the shrine area. Keep noise levels down. |\\n| **Late Afternoon** | Continue ascending as far as you feel comfortable. The crowds thin out the higher you go. | Included | Time your descent for the soft evening light. |\\n| **Evening** | Dinner and local exploration near your accommodation or in the lively Pontocho Alley. | 2 hours | **Dining Suggestion:** Try *Kushikatsu* (deep-fried skewers) in Pontocho. |\\n\\n### Day 2: The Heart of Tradition (Eastern Kyoto / Higashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Early Morning** | Visit **Kiyomizu-dera Temple**. Go right at opening (usually 6:00 AM or 9:00 AM depending on season). | 1.5 hours | Essential to beat crowds. Enjoy the view from the wooden stage. |\\n| **Late Morning** | Walk the historic streets of **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka**. | 2 hours | **Activity:** Browse traditional crafts, pottery, and sweets (e.g., Yatsuhashi). Stop for a matcha soft-serve. |\\n| **Lunch** | Traditional set lunch (*Teishoku*) in Higashiyama. | 1 hour | **Dining Suggestion:** Look for restaurants offering *obanzai* (Kyoto home cooking). |\\n| **Afternoon** | Explore **Kodai-ji Temple** (known for its beautiful gardens) or visit the nearby **Yasaka Shrine**. | 2 hours | Quiet reflection time after the busy main streets. |\\n| **Evening** | Stroll through the **Gion District** (Hanami-koji street). | 2 hours | **Etiquette Reminder:** Do not stop or accost Maiko/Geiko for photos. Observe respectfully from a distance. |\\n| **Dinner** | Enjoy *Kyoto-style Kaiseki* (traditional multi-course dinner) if budget allows, or look for specialized eel restaurants (*unagi*). | 2 hours | **Reservation Highly Recommended for Kaiseki.** |\\n\\n### Day 3: Zen, Gold, and Contemplation (Northwest Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel to **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)**. Visit immediately upon opening. | 1.5 hours | The reflection across the pond is best viewed in the morning sun. |\\n| **Midday** | Travel to **Ryoan-ji Temple**. Contemplate the famous rock garden. | 1.5 hours | **Culture Focus:** Spend time sitting silently, observing the garden\u2019s abstract design. |\\n| **Lunch** | Vegetarian Buddhist cuisine (*Shojin Ryori*) near the temples. | 1 hour | **Dining Suggestion:** Look for *Shojin Ryori* restaurants around the Daitoku-ji complex for an authentic Zen meal. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Explore the **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex**. This area is known for its many sub-temples and links to tea culture. | 2.5 hours | **Optional:** Visit a smaller sub-temple for a quiet break. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to Central Kyoto. Casual dinner and early rest after a morning of travel. | 2 hours | **Dining Suggestion:** Explore the Nishiki Market area for small, quick eats before they close, or grab Udon noodles. |\\n\\n### Day 4: Nature Immersion in Arashiyama (Western Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Early Morning** | Take the JR Sagano Line to Saga-Arashiyama Station. Head straight to the **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove**. | 1.5 hours | **Crucial Tip:** Arrive before 8:30 AM for the best, quietest experience. |\\n| **Mid-Morning** | Visit **Tenryu-ji Temple**, exploring its extensive, beautiful strolling garden. | 1.5 hours | The garden connects directly to the Bamboo Grove path. |\\n| **Lunch** | Lunch near the Katsura River. | 1 hour | **Dining Suggestion:** Try restaurants specializing in Tofu dishes, a regional specialty. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Walk across the **Togetsukyo Bridge**. Visit **Okochi Sanso Villa** for stunning panoramic garden views (requires a small hike). | 2.5 hours | The villa entry includes tea, making it a pleasant break. |\\n| **Late Afternoon** | Optional: Take the scenic **Sagano Romantic Train** (seasonal) or visit the quiet **Adashino Nenbutsu-ji Temple**. | 2 hours |\\n| **Dinner** | Dinner back in central Kyoto or near your hotel. | 1.5 hours | Try *Okonomiyaki* (savory pancake) for a hearty, fun meal. |\\n\\n### Day 5: Imperial History and Philosophical Reflection\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Visit the **Kyoto Imperial Palace (Kyoto Gosho)** grounds. (Check reservation requirements, though often grounds are open access). | 2 hours | Reflect on the sheer scale of the former seat of power. |\\n| **Late Morning** | Travel to the **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku no Michi)**. | 1 hour | Start at the northern end near Ginkaku-ji and walk south. |\\n| **Lunch** | Simple lunch along the Path or near the southern end. | 1 hour | Grab a *bento box* to enjoy riverside if the weather is nice. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Visit **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)**. Focus on its unique dry sand garden (*Ginshadan*). | 1.5 hours | A contrast to Kinkaku-ji\u2014celebrating subtle beauty (*wabi-sabi*). |\\n| **Late Afternoon** | Explore the **Heian Jingu Shrine**, known for its immense bright red *torii* gate and vast garden. | 1.5 hours |\\n| **Dinner** | Explore the *depachika* (basement food halls) of a major department store (like Takashimaya) for an incredible variety of high-quality takeout. | 2 hours | A fantastic, high-quality budget dinner option. |\\n\\n### Day 6: Shoguns, Tea Ceremony Immersion\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Visit **Nijo Castle**. Focus on the Ninomaru Palace and its \\\"nightingale floors\\\" (squeaking security mechanism). | 2.5 hours | **Culture Focus:** Observe the opulent decoration symbolizing Shogun power, contrasted with the austerity of the temples. |\\n| **Lunch** | Quick lunch near Nijo Castle or back toward the city center. | 1 hour |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Dedicated Tea Ceremony Experience.** (Pre-booked essential). | 2 \u2013 3 hours | **Highlight:** Choose a ceremony focused on the philosophy behind *Chanoyu*. Follow all instructions from the host meticulously. |\\n| **Late Afternoon** | **Optional:** Visit **Kennin-ji Temple** (oldest Zen temple in Kyoto) near Gion for its beautiful Zen artwork. | 1.5 hours |\\n| **Dinner** | Farewell dinner reflecting your favorite Kyoto cuisine discovered this week (e.g., return to a favorite ramen spot or try a specialized *shabu-shabu*). | 2 hours | Use this evening to practice a few Japanese phrases learned during the week. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Matcha Pilgrimage and Departure (Uji Day Trip)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Est. Time | Dining & Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel via Keihan or JR Nara Line to **Uji City** (approx. 20-30 minutes from Kyoto). | 1 hour | Uji is the center of premium matcha production. |\\n| **Late Morning** | Visit **Byodo-in Temple** (featured on the 10-yen coin). | 1.5 hours | Admire the Phoenix Hall architecture. |\\n| **Lunch** | Lunch centered entirely around **Matcha**. | 1.5 hours | **Dining Suggestion:** Try *Matcha Soba* noodles or rich *Matcha Parfaits* at a famous local tea house (e.g., Nakamura Tokichi Honten). |\\n| **Afternoon** | Purchase high-quality loose-leaf tea or matcha souvenirs in Uji. Enjoy a final, calming cup of expertly whisked tea. | 2 hours |\\n| **Late Afternoon/Evening**| Return to Kyoto Station. Final souvenir shopping or direct transit to the airport for departure. | Varies | Allow ample time for airport transfer. |\",\"__llm_request_key__\":\"201ad818-aacd-49ad-9951-74ebd78ef4f8\",\"destination_research\":\"Excellent! Kyoto is the perfect destination for a cultural deep-dive. Here is a comprehensive plan incorporating temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature for your 7-day experience.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto: Temples, Traditions, Tea & Nature\\n\\n### 1. Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto for pleasant weather are **Spring (March to May)** and **Autumn (October to November)**.\\n\\n*   **Spring (March-May):** Famous for the beautiful cherry blossoms (Sakura), typically peaking from late March to mid-April. Temperatures are mild, ranging from cool in March to warm by May. **Caution:** This is peak season, meaning large crowds and higher prices, especially during Golden Week (late April/early May).\\n*   **Autumn (October-November):** Known for spectacular fall foliage, peaking in November. November often offers some of the best weather\u2014sunny, crisp, and drier, with average highs around 17\u00b0C (62\u00b0F). This is also a very busy and expensive time.\\n*   **General Temple Visits:** Major sights are best visited early in the morning or late in the day to avoid midday crowds. Most temples are typically open from around 9 am to 5 pm, though hours can vary seasonally.\\n\\n### 2. Top Attractions & Must-See Locations (Thematic Grouping)\\n\\nThis itinerary focuses on key cultural areas that blend your interests.\\n\\n| Day | Theme/Area | Key Sites (Temples, Nature, Culture) |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **1** | **Arrival & Southern Icons** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine:** Famous for thousands of vermilion *torii* gates winding up a mountain trail (nature/spirituality). Go early to avoid crowds. |\\n| **2** | **Eastern Beauty & Tradition** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple:** UNESCO site with a famous wooden stage overlooking the city. **Higashiyama District (Sannenzaka/Ninenzaka):** Wander preserved historic streets with traditional shops and tea houses. **Gion:** Kyoto's famous geisha district, ideal for evening strolls. |\\n| **3** | **Zen, Gold, and Rock Gardens** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion):** Iconic Zen temple covered in gold leaf. **Ryoan-ji Temple:** Home to Japan\u2019s most famous rock garden (Zen/Nature). **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex:** Associated with the art of tea ceremonies. |\\n| **4** | **Arashiyama: Nature Immersion** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove:** Walk through the serene, towering bamboo forest (Nature). **Tenryu-ji Temple:** Explore its significant 14th-century Zen stroll garden. **Okochi Sanso Villa:** Stunning garden vistas (Nature). |\\n| **5** | **Imperial History & Eastern Serenity** | **Kyoto Imperial Palace:** Explore the former residence of the Emperor. **Philosopher's Path:** A pleasant canal-side walk, beautiful in all seasons. **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion):** Known for its dry sand garden. |\\n| **6** | **Deep Tradition & Tea Focus** | **Nijo Castle:** Explore the former residence of the Shogun with its famous \\\"nightingale floors.\\\" **Tea Ceremony Experience:** Dedicate the afternoon to a deep, authentic tea ceremony (see below). **Optional:** Visit Kennin-ji Temple (oldest Zen temple, has a teahouse). |\\n| **7** | **Matcha Culture & Departure** | **Day Trip to Uji:** Known as the birthplace of high-quality Japanese green tea (Matcha). Visit Byodo-in Temple (featured on the 10 yen coin) and enjoy specialty tea houses. |\\n\\n### 3. Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\nRespecting local customs is key for a cultural deep-dive:\\n\\n*   **Temples & Shrines:**\\n    *   **Bowing:** Bow slightly when entering temple grounds as a sign of respect.\\n    *   **Shoes:** Be prepared to remove your shoes before entering temple buildings or tatami rooms; slip-on shoes are convenient. Bring a small bag to carry them if necessary.\\n    *   **Conduct:** Keep your voice down, avoid running, and do not eat or drink in sacred areas.\\n    *   **Photography:** Observe signs carefully; photography may be prohibited or flash use restricted. Do not photograph worshippers without permission.\\n    *   **Purification:** Use the *temizuya* (purification fountain) correctly: use the ladle with your right hand for your left, then your right, then rinse your mouth (don't touch the ladle to your lips), and finally rinse the ladle handle before returning it.\\n*   **General Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is not a custom in Japan and is generally not appreciated. A simple polite phrase like *\\\"Okini\\\"* (Thank you in Kansai dialect) is sufficient.\\n    *   **Queuing:** Always line up neatly when waiting for buses, trains, or shops.\\n    *   **Maiko/Geiko:** Do not stop, bother, or jostle *Maiko* (apprentice geisha) or *Geiko* (Kyoto term for Geisha) for photos in districts like Gion; they are often busy.\\n    *   **Language:** Using courteous Japanese expressions like *\\\"Arigat\u014d gozaimasu\\\"* (thank you very much) and *\\\"Sumimasen\\\"* (pardon me/sorry) shows respect.\\n\\n### 4. Transportation Options\\n\\nKyoto has an excellent, though sometimes crowded, public transport network, which is the most convenient way to navigate.\\n\\n*   **Buses (Recommended for Sightseeing):** The bus network is extensive and connects directly to most major attractions where trains/subways don't reach. **Tip:** Enter through the back door and exit through the front, paying the fixed fare to the driver as you get off. A **Kyoto Bus Pass** (approx. 600 yen for a day) is the cheapest way for extensive travel.\\n*   **Subways & Trains:** Kyoto has two subway lines (Karasuma running North-South, Tozai running East-West) and several private railway lines (JR, Hankyu, Keihan) useful for day trips (like to Nara or Arashiyama).\\n*   **IC Cards:** Purchasing an IC card (like ICOCA, Suica, or Pasmo) is highly recommended as they work on virtually all buses and trains in Kyoto (and across Japan).\\n*   **Walking/Bicycling:** The city center, Arashiyama, and Higashiyama are great for walking. Bicycles are also a worthwhile alternative as the city is relatively flat.\\n*   **Taxis:** Plentiful and reliable, but more expensive than public transport. Remember that many taxis only accept cash.\\n\\n### 5. Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** Kyoto is generally a very safe city.\\n*   **Walking/Street Conduct:** Do not litter (fines can be high). Be mindful of narrow streets and stand clear of the curb when taxis arrive, as their rear doors open automatically.\\n*   **Smoking:** Smoking while walking is often frowned upon and prohibited in many public areas, sometimes incurring a fine if done outside designated zones.\\n*   **Reservations:** Many popular tea ceremony experiences require advance reservation, sometimes months ahead during peak seasons.\\n\\n### **Deep Dive Highlight: Tea Ceremony Experience**\\n\\nFor your cultural focus, aim for an **authentic tea ceremony (*Chanoyu* or *Sad\u014d*)**:\\n\\n*   **Experience Types:** You can choose between a *Chakai* (informal gathering, about 45-60 minutes) or a *Chaji* (formal gathering, lasting 3-4 hours with a meal).\\n*   **Locations:** Experiences are offered in traditional *machiya* (townhouses), teahouses, or even Zen temples (which often focus on the meditation aspect).\\n*   **Bonus:** Some locations offer kimono rental to enhance the experience.\"},\"events\":[{\"model_version\":null,\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"grounding_metadata\":null,\"partial\":null,\"turn_complete\":null,\"finish_reason\":null,\"error_code\":null,\"error_message\":null,\"interrupted\":null,\"custom_metadata\":null,\"usage_metadata\":null,\"live_session_resumption_update\":null,\"input_transcription\":null,\"output_transcription\":null,\"avg_logprobs\":null,\"logprobs_result\":null,\"cache_metadata\":null,\"citation_metadata\":null,\"invocation_id\":\"e-1b6fb806-c081-44e0-b38a-4911b334db1d\",\"author\":\"user\",\"actions\":{\"skip_summarization\":null,\"state_delta\":{},\"artifact_delta\":{},\"transfer_to_agent\":null,\"escalate\":null,\"requested_auth_configs\":{},\"requested_tool_confirmations\":{},\"compaction\":null,\"end_of_agent\":null,\"agent_state\":null,\"rewind_before_invocation_id\":null},\"long_running_tool_ids\":null,\"branch\":null,\"id\":\"67cedd5f-8b95-4663-8a95-2c025bb3a57e\",\"timestamp\":1763640809.270647},{\"model_version\":\"gemini-2.5-flash-lite-preview-09-2025\",\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Excellent! Kyoto is the perfect destination for a cultural deep-dive. Here is a comprehensive plan incorporating temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature for your 7-day experience.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto: Temples, Traditions, Tea & Nature\\n\\n### 1. Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto for pleasant weather are **Spring (March to May)** and **Autumn (October to November)**.\\n\\n*   **Spring (March-May):** Famous for the beautiful cherry blossoms (Sakura), typically peaking from late March to mid-April. Temperatures are mild, ranging from cool in March to warm by May. **Caution:** This is peak season, meaning large crowds and higher prices, especially during Golden Week (late April/early May).\\n*   **Autumn (October-November):** Known for spectacular fall foliage, peaking in November. November often offers some of the best weather\u2014sunny, crisp, and drier, with average highs around 17\u00b0C (62\u00b0F). This is also a very busy and expensive time.\\n*   **General Temple Visits:** Major sights are best visited early in the morning or late in the day to avoid midday crowds. Most temples are typically open from around 9 am to 5 pm, though hours can vary seasonally.\\n\\n### 2. Top Attractions & Must-See Locations (Thematic Grouping)\\n\\nThis itinerary focuses on key cultural areas that blend your interests.\\n\\n| Day | Theme/Area | Key Sites (Temples, Nature, Culture) |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **1** | **Arrival & Southern Icons** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine:** Famous for thousands of vermilion *torii* gates winding up a mountain trail (nature/spirituality). Go early to avoid crowds. |\\n| **2** | **Eastern Beauty & Tradition** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple:** UNESCO site with a famous wooden stage overlooking the city. **Higashiyama District (Sannenzaka/Ninenzaka):** Wander preserved historic streets with traditional shops and tea houses. **Gion:** Kyoto's famous geisha district, ideal for evening strolls. |\\n| **3** | **Zen, Gold, and Rock Gardens** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion):** Iconic Zen temple covered in gold leaf. **Ryoan-ji Temple:** Home to Japan\u2019s most famous rock garden (Zen/Nature). **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex:** Associated with the art of tea ceremonies. |\\n| **4** | **Arashiyama: Nature Immersion** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove:** Walk through the serene, towering bamboo forest (Nature). **Tenryu-ji Temple:** Explore its significant 14th-century Zen stroll garden. **Okochi Sanso Villa:** Stunning garden vistas (Nature). |\\n| **5** | **Imperial History & Eastern Serenity** | **Kyoto Imperial Palace:** Explore the former residence of the Emperor. **Philosopher's Path:** A pleasant canal-side walk, beautiful in all seasons. **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion):** Known for its dry sand garden. |\\n| **6** | **Deep Tradition & Tea Focus** | **Nijo Castle:** Explore the former residence of the Shogun with its famous \\\"nightingale floors.\\\" **Tea Ceremony Experience:** Dedicate the afternoon to a deep, authentic tea ceremony (see below). **Optional:** Visit Kennin-ji Temple (oldest Zen temple, has a teahouse). |\\n| **7** | **Matcha Culture & Departure** | **Day Trip to Uji:** Known as the birthplace of high-quality Japanese green tea (Matcha). Visit Byodo-in Temple (featured on the 10 yen coin) and enjoy specialty tea houses. |\\n\\n### 3. Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\nRespecting local customs is key for a cultural deep-dive:\\n\\n*   **Temples & Shrines:**\\n    *   **Bowing:** Bow slightly when entering temple grounds as a sign of respect.\\n    *   **Shoes:** Be prepared to remove your shoes before entering temple buildings or tatami rooms; slip-on shoes are convenient. Bring a small bag to carry them if necessary.\\n    *   **Conduct:** Keep your voice down, avoid running, and do not eat or drink in sacred areas.\\n    *   **Photography:** Observe signs carefully; photography may be prohibited or flash use restricted. Do not photograph worshippers without permission.\\n    *   **Purification:** Use the *temizuya* (purification fountain) correctly: use the ladle with your right hand for your left, then your right, then rinse your mouth (don't touch the ladle to your lips), and finally rinse the ladle handle before returning it.\\n*   **General Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is not a custom in Japan and is generally not appreciated. A simple polite phrase like *\\\"Okini\\\"* (Thank you in Kansai dialect) is sufficient.\\n    *   **Queuing:** Always line up neatly when waiting for buses, trains, or shops.\\n    *   **Maiko/Geiko:** Do not stop, bother, or jostle *Maiko* (apprentice geisha) or *Geiko* (Kyoto term for Geisha) for photos in districts like Gion; they are often busy.\\n    *   **Language:** Using courteous Japanese expressions like *\\\"Arigat\u014d gozaimasu\\\"* (thank you very much) and *\\\"Sumimasen\\\"* (pardon me/sorry) shows respect.\\n\\n### 4. Transportation Options\\n\\nKyoto has an excellent, though sometimes crowded, public transport network, which is the most convenient way to navigate.\\n\\n*   **Buses (Recommended for Sightseeing):** The bus network is extensive and connects directly to most major attractions where trains/subways don't reach. **Tip:** Enter through the back door and exit through the front, paying the fixed fare to the driver as you get off. A **Kyoto Bus Pass** (approx. 600 yen for a day) is the cheapest way for extensive travel.\\n*   **Subways & Trains:** Kyoto has two subway lines (Karasuma running North-South, Tozai running East-West) and several private railway lines (JR, Hankyu, Keihan) useful for day trips (like to Nara or Arashiyama).\\n*   **IC Cards:** Purchasing an IC card (like ICOCA, Suica, or Pasmo) is highly recommended as they work on virtually all buses and trains in Kyoto (and across Japan).\\n*   **Walking/Bicycling:** The city center, Arashiyama, and Higashiyama are great for walking. Bicycles are also a worthwhile alternative as the city is relatively flat.\\n*   **Taxis:** Plentiful and reliable, but more expensive than public transport. Remember that many taxis only accept cash.\\n\\n### 5. Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** Kyoto is generally a very safe city.\\n*   **Walking/Street Conduct:** Do not litter (fines can be high). Be mindful of narrow streets and stand clear of the curb when taxis arrive, as their rear doors open automatically.\\n*   **Smoking:** Smoking while walking is often frowned upon and prohibited in many public areas, sometimes incurring a fine if done outside designated zones.\\n*   **Reservations:** Many popular tea ceremony experiences require advance reservation, sometimes months ahead during peak seasons.\\n\\n### **Deep Dive Highlight: Tea Ceremony Experience**\\n\\nFor your cultural focus, aim for an **authentic tea ceremony (*Chanoyu* or *Sad\u014d*)**:\\n\\n*   **Experience Types:** You can choose between a *Chakai* (informal gathering, about 45-60 minutes) or a *Chaji* (formal gathering, lasting 3-4 hours with a meal).\\n*   **Locations:** Experiences are offered in traditional *machiya* (townhouses), teahouses, or even Zen temples (which often focus on the meditation aspect).\\n*   **Bonus:** Some locations offer kimono rental to enhance the experience.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"grounding_metadata\":{\"google_maps_widget_context_token\":null,\"grounding_chunks\":[{\"maps\":null,\"retrieved_context\":null,\"web\":{\"domain\":null,\"title\":\"santorinidave.com\",\"uri\":\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGf2jzcTNWk5z10iijOgaNs-dwcoZFCr2H4T8PtCtazryDCQYyK9jM5FiUSGhgf-B-6_Z9Zb1LVQRuZluQ8FIQzLVPtaX1FAfbrh8RuXcTo2oRglo2pZVVpCDSeHmrWNr25_4iYv03ymVyoo_25\"}},{\"maps\":null,\"retrieved_context\":null,\"web\":{\"domain\":null,\"title\":\"japanhighlights.com\",\"uri\":\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEO0lChjbUufyLuA5Q9ZrbPEK06GgAUEJYvRklstoD-5YOAb5jCR9QUvkS_w8vRZZAeSIjUcTYD1Kn7LF0ODX0cdAvB6llfG0EVVTxNU6T8pK0ee6f8ef32eFJBhcYKroBQeGNSH6X41DuBf2yAJ08kE8A1_letIGqM\"}},{\"maps\":null,\"retrieved_context\":null,\"web\":{\"domain\":null,\"title\":\"insidekyoto.com\",\"uri\":\"https://vertexaisearch